|> Exercise
    handle=_dimensional_f_f_

    For functions $f$ with what kind of
    signature does the composition
    $$
    f \circ f
    $$
    make sense?

    |> Solution

        A signature of the form 
        $$
        f : [\tA] \ra [\tA]
        $$
        is necessary and sufficient. In more detail, for  
        two functions $f$, $g$ of general signature

        $$
        \begin{gathered}
        f : [\tA] \ra [\tB] \\
        \up{1.6}g : [\tC] \ra [\tD]
        \end{gathered}
        $$

        the composition

        $$
        f\circ g
        $$

        is well-formed if and ony if $[\tD] = [\tA]$. 
        (Cf. [Table 1.1](>>table_1_1).)
        In our case $g = f$ so we additionally have

        $$
        \begin{gathered}
        !! some mathjax bug requires this \up{0}, on last check:
        \up{0}[\tC] = [\tA] \\
        \up{1.6}[\tD] = [\tB]
        \end{gathered}
        $$

        to start with, which, together with 
        $[\tD] = [\tA]$, collapses the signature of $f$
        to “$[\tA] \ra [\tA]$”.

        !! The output of $f$ is fed back to into itself
        !! as an input, so a signature of the form

        !! $$
        !! f : [\tA] \ra [\tA]
        !! $$

        !! is required, and is also sufficient by inspection!

|> Exercise
    handle=_sueeyyy_

    !! For wich functions $f$ (with what kind of
    !! signature) does the function
    What constraints does the assemblage
    $$
    f \circ {f\over f\circ f}
    $$
    !! make sense?
    impose on the signature of $f$?

    |> Solution

        To start with, the presence of $f \circ f$ as a sub-expression
        imposes a restricted signature of the form

        $$
        f : [\tA] \ra [\tA]
        $$

        by Exercise >>_dimensional_f_f_. But then

        $$
        {f\over f\circ f} : [\tA] \ra [1]
        $$

        !! $$
        !! f : [\tA] \ra [\tA]
        !! $$
        !! $$
        !! f\circ f : [\tA] \ra [\tA]
        !! $$
        !! $$
        !! {f\over f\circ f} : [\tA] \ra \left[{\tA\over \tA}\right] = [\tA] \ra [1]
        !! $$

        because the output units at top and bottom of the fraction are
        both A, which cancels. (Or

        |> Image
            src=images/e6_some_quotient.svg

        where $\tA/\tA = 1$.) As

        $$
        {f\over f\circ f}
        $$

        is fed back to $f$, this forces...

        $$
        [\tA] = [1]
        $$

        ...$f$ to accept dimensionless inputs, and...
        
        $$f : [1] \ra [1]$$

        ..._in fine_.

        !! Because
        !! $$
        !! f\circ f
        !! $$
        !! has the same output units as $f$, the fraction
        !! $$
        !! {f\over f\circ f}
        !! $$
        !! outputs a dimensionless quantity (same units over
        !! same units); but the value of this fra


|> Exercise
    handle=_dimensional_f_f_f_

    !! For what kinds of function $f$ (what kinds of
    !! signature) does
    For functions $f$ with what kind of
    signature does the composition
    $$
    \Large f \circ f \circ f
    $$
    make sense?

    |> Solution

        A signature of the form

        $$
        f : [\tA] \ra [\tA]
        $$

        is necessary and sufficient. In more detail,
        the composition

        $$
        f \circ f
        $$

        already requires a signature of this form 
        by Exercise >>_dimensional_f_f_, after which

        $$
        f \circ f : [\tA] \ra [\tA]
        $$

        and

        $$
        f \circ f \circ f = f \circ (f \circ f)
        $$

        is also well-formed, since

        $$
        \begin{gathered}
        f : [\tA] \ra [\tA] \\
        \up{1.6}f \circ f : [\tA] \ra [\tA]
        \end{gathered}
        $$

        can be used in lieu of $f$ and $g$ in a general
        composition $f \circ g$.

|> Exercise
    handle=_dimensional_f_g_f_

    !! What restrictions on the signature of $f$ and $g$
    !! does the composition
    !! $$
    !! \Large f \circ g \circ f
    !! $$
    !! impose?
    What kinds of functions $f$ and $g$, signature-wise,
    does the composition 
    $$
    f \circ g \circ f
    $$
    require?
    !! For functions $f$ and $g$ with what kind of signature 
    !! does the composition
    !! $$
    !! \Large f \circ g \circ f
    !! $$
    !! make sense?

    |> Solution

        The composition requires $f$ and $g$ of the form

        $$
        \begin{gathered}
        f : [\tA] \ra [\tB] \\
        \up{1.6}g : [\tB] \ra [\tA]
        \end{gathered}
        $$

        for some units A and B
        since the output of $f$ is forwarded to $g$, whose
        output of $g$ if forwarded back to $f$.

|> Exercise
    handle=_physicist_choose_to_write_

    If units of time and space are chosen
    such that $c = 1$ where $c$ is the speed of the light,
    why might we still choose
    to write expressions containing ‘$c$’?

    |> Solution

        !! Besides wanting to “remind the general
        !! formula”, one reason is
        !! to keep expressions dimensionally consistent;
        !! for example, if $v < c$
        !! is another speed, then

        !! $$
        !! \sqrt{1 - {v^2\over c^2}}
        !! $$

        !! is the so-called _Lorentz contraction factor_
        !! of special relativity, which is dimensionless—writing

        !! $$
        !! \sqrt{1 - v^2}
        !! $$

        !! might be numerically correct and typographically more
        !! expedient, but is dimensionally
        !! inconsistent: either a speed squared is being subtracted
        !! from a dimensionless ‘$1$’, or else we pretend
        !! that ‘$1$’ stands for ‘$c^2$’, which is also a speed squared,
        !! but then the entire square root is a speed instead of
        !! being dimensionless—failures on all sides!

        Besides wanting to “remind the general
        formula”, one reason is
        to keep expressions dimensionally consistent.
        For example, the reciprocal of the Lorentz factor

        $$
        \sqrt{1 - {v^2\over c^2}}
        $$

        (cf. Exercise >>_lorentz_factor_dimensionality_)
        !! is the so-called _Lorentz factor_
        !! of special relativity, which is dimensionless—writing
        would become

        $$
        \sqrt{1 - v^2}
        $$

        if 
        taking advantage of $c = 1$ to
        elide the ‘$c$’, which
        might be numerically correct and typographically more
        expedient, but is dimensionally
        inconsistent: either a speed squared is being subtracted
        from a dimensionless ‘$1$’, or, in a last-ditch effort, we pretend
        that ‘$1$’ stands for ‘$c^2$’, which is also a speed squared,
        but then the entire square root is a speed instead of
        being dimensionless, as the Lorentz factor should be—failures on all sides!



|> Exercise
    handle=_dimensional_analysis_of_product_rule_
    Give a dimensional analysis of the (real,
    “late afternoon”) product rule (cf$.$ Chapter >>derivatives#decoy:4, Exercise >>_product_rule_#decoy:16):

    |> Boxed
        $$
        \Large (fg)' = f'g + fg'
        $$

    |> Solution
        Products of the form

        !! $$
        !! fg
        !! $$
        !! $$
        !! f'g
        !! $$
        !! $$
        !! fg'
        !! $$

        $$
        \begin{gathered}
        fg  \\
        f'g\up{1.9} \\
        fg'\up{1.9}
        \end{gathered}
        $$

        require $f$ and $g$ to have the same input units, but no more,
        per the multiplication rule of [Table 1.1](>>table_1_1),
        and per the fact that
        $h'$ has the same input units as $h$
        for any function $h : \rr \ra \rr$. 
        Therefore, signatures of the form

        $$
        \begin{gathered}
        f : [\tA] \ra [\tB] \\
        g : [\tA] \ra [\tC] \up{1.6}
        \end{gathered}
        $$

        are at least necessary on either side of the equation.
        Then, evaluating further, one finds
        !! But then one finds, in order, that
        !! $$
        !! \begin{gathered}
        !! fg : [\tA] \ra [\tB\tC] \\
        !! \up{2.5}(fg)' : [\tA] \ra \left[{\tB\tC\over \tA}\right] \\
        !! \up{2.5}f' : [\tA] \ra \left[{\tB\over \tA}\right] \\
        !! \up{2.5}f'g : [\tA] \ra \left[{\tB\tC\over \tA}\right] \\
        !! \up{2.5}g' : [\tA] \ra \left[{\tC\over \tA}\right] \\
        !! \up{2.5}fg' : [\tA] \ra \left[{\tB\tC\over \tA}\right]
        !! \end{gathered}
        !! $$

        $$
        \begin{gathered}
        fg : [\tA] \ra [\tB\tC]                    \\
        \up{1.6}(fg)' : [\tA] \ra [\tB\tC/\tA]     \\
        \up{1.6}f' : [\tA] \ra [\tB/\tA]           \\
        \up{1.6}g' : [\tA] \ra [\tC/\tA]           \\
        \up{1.6}f'g : [\tA] \ra [\tB\tC/\tA]       \\
        \up{1.6}fg' : [\tA] \ra [\tB\tC/\tA]       \\
        \up{1.6}f'g + fg' : [\tA] \ra [\tB\tC/\tA]
        \end{gathered}
        $$

        so these signatures are also sufficient to
        evaluate either side of the equation to the end, and,
        moreover,
        $(fg)'$
        and
        $f'g + g'f$
        are found to have the same signature, concluding the 
        dimensional analysis. (= Both sides impose the same
        signature constraints, and evaluate to the same final
        signature.)

        !! so signatures as above are also _sufficient_ to
        !! evaluate either side of the equation to the end, and,
        !! moreover,
        !! $(fg)'$
        !! and
        !! $f'g + g'f$
        !! are found to have the same signature,
        !! so the equation is dimensionally consistent.
